# DTF tier manifest: vvc3-scoped-admin  (GHSA-vvc3, 1.6.1 security release)
# Profile-set: filesystem / single (the walking-skeleton topology; the auth
# defect this tier gates is storage-independent).
#
# The bug (GHSA-vvc3): an API token inherits its OWNER's `is_admin` flag
# regardless of the token's own scopes. A READ-scoped Personal Access Token
# minted by an admin therefore sails through the admin gate (`admin_middleware`
# checks only `is_admin`, never the token's action-scope ceiling). Any admin who
# hands a "read-only" automation token to CI has, unknowingly, handed out full
# admin: that token can create users, install plugins, mutate signing keys.
#
# The fix (1.6.1): admin routes require the presenting credential to actually
# CARRY an admin-class scope (`admin` / `*`). Interactive logins and CI/federated
# JWTs (scopes = None, action-unrestricted) keep full admin; an API token is held
# to its minted scope allowlist, so a read-scoped admin token is refused with 403.
#
# Oracle (discriminating, RELEASE_GATE=1):
#   (A) BOUNDARY  a READ-scoped (`read:artifacts`) API token owned by an admin,
#       on the admin routes POST /api/v1/users and GET /api/v1/users -> 403, and
#       the DB shows the create never happened. On the baseline this returns 2xx
#       and the user row IS created (the privilege-escalation bypass = RED).
#   (B) POSITIVE  an ADMIN-scoped (`*`) API token owned by the same admin on the
#       SAME route -> 2xx (admin automation still works), and an interactive
#       admin Bearer JWT -> 2xx. Proves the fix is scope-aware, not a blanket
#       "API tokens can never be admin" hammer, and the pipeline still grants
#       real admin (the tier is not a hollow always-403).
#   (C) NEGATIVE  a plain non-admin JWT on the same admin route -> 403. Proves
#       the admin gate itself is intact.
#
# RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED=false: the oracle drives an admin login + two token mints +
# a user create + a user login + several back-to-back admin-route probes through
# one admin/user; the base default (ON) would throttle them. A tier that must
# disable a shipped control does so explicitly (design 2.2, same as the isolation
# / ci-token-basic / sso tiers).
PROFILES="storage.filesystem"
ORACLE="oracle.sh"
RATE_LIMIT_ENABLED="false"
